Article 33
(1) The fulfillment of children's rights is implemented in an integrated and systematic manner from all sectors on an ongoing basis through the KLA development policy.
- 15- (2) The KLA development policy as referred to in paragraph (1) contains provisions regarding:
a. the KLA concept;
b. children's rights; and
c. the KLA development approach.
(3) The KLA development policy as referred to in paragraph (1) is directed towards the fulfillment of children's rights, including:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, utilization of leisure time, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.
(4) The stages of KLA development as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. preparation;
b. planning;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.
(5) Further provisions regarding the KLA development policy shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 2
Protection of women and children victims of violence is conducted based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims; and
e. empowerment.

Article 34
(1) In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of KLA development policy in the Region, a KLA Task Force shall be established. (2) The KLA Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1), shall have the following principal duties, namely:
a. coordinating the implementation of KLA policy and development;
b. determining the tasks of the members of the KLA Task Force; c. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication of information and education on KLA policy;
d. collecting baseline data;
e. conducting needs analysis sourced from baseline data;
f. conducting dissemination of baseline data;
g. determining the focus and priorities of programs in realizing KLA, which shall be adjusted to the potential of the Region;
h. preparing a regional action plan and KLA working mechanism for 5 (five) years; and
i. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least 1 (one) time per year.
(3) Further provisions regarding the procedures for appointment and dismissal of membership of the KLA Task Force shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 23
(1) Health Services as referred to in Article 20 letter c constitute efforts that include promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative aspects for both basic health services and referral.
(2) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. first aid to victims;
b. examination and treatment;
c. follow-up examination of victims;
d. coordination of health rehabilitation implementation;
e. medico-legal examination including collection of evidence from victims and preparation of visum et repertum;
f. supporting examination and laboratory testing of evidence;
g. specialist doctor consultation or making referrals; and
h. case reporting.

Article 2
Protection of women and children victims of violence is conducted based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims; and
e. empowerment.

Article 27
(1) The provision of services to victims as referred to in Article 20 shall be implemented with the principles of:
a. swift;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. not subject to fees; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.
(2) Service providers or service managers are prohibited from collecting any fees from victims.
